    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d194">

                <pb facs="0307" n="209" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg209"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.5521/11</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Belgium</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Whitlock</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Brussels</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-03-22"><hi rendition="#italic">March 22,
                        1921</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received April 6.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1157</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the Department’s
                    instruction no. 615 of November 1920, and to my despatch no. 1060 of December
                    14th last,<anchor n="68" xml:id="d194-f1"/> relative to the termination of the
                    Treaty of Amity, Commerce and Navigation concluded on January 24, 1891, between
                    the United States and the Independent State of the Congo, I have the honour to
                    report that I have not as yet received an answer from the Foreign Office to my
                    communication of December 13th.<anchor n="69" xml:id="d194-f2"/></p>
                <p>In the official <hi rendition="#italic">Moniteur Belge</hi> of March 19th,
                    however, there appears the following notice: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p rendition="#flush">“Treaty of Commerce between the Independent State of
                            the Congo and the United States of America.</p>
                        <p>The Government of the United States of America has informed the Belgian
                            Government of the denunciation of the Treaty of Commerce signed January
                            24, 1891, between the Free State of the Congo and the United States of
                            America.</p>
                        <p>The above mentioned treaty ceased to be operative on July 1, 1917.</p>
                        <p>Certified to by the Secretary General of the Ministry of Foreign
                            Affiars.</p>
                        <p rendition="#right">(signed) H. Costermans.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Brand Whitlock</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d194-f1" n="68" xml:id="d194-f1-content">
                <p>Latter not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d194-f2" n="69" xml:id="d194-f2-content">
                <p>See Department’s instruction no. 615, Nov. 19, 1920, to the Ambassador in
                    Belgium, <hi rendition="#italic">supra.</hi></p>
            </note>
